{"A":"Research a parallel comparison-sort candidate for the generated array, such as task-based quicksort. Keep case generation unchanged and serial; avoid races on the source’s global PRNG by using private pivot state or another output-independent pivot strategy. Limit work to the available four cores, tune task cutoffs across input sizes, and verify sortedness and checksum correctness.","B":"Research a parallel radix-sort candidate for signed 32-bit integers. Use signed-order-preserving keys and per-worker histograms/prefix offsets so scatter is race-free; handle small arrays without unnecessary parallel overhead. Keep generation and MD5 behavior unchanged, and verify correctness across input sizes rather than relying on a particular generated case.","C":"Research a serial comparison-sort improvement, such as introsort with tail recursion, a small-partition insertion-sort cutoff, and a heapsort fallback. Compare sensible pivot/partition choices, while preserving signed integer ordering and the existing generated values, output format, and checksum calculation.","G":"Evaluate A, B, and C’s complete candidates against the valid baseline using correctness checks and end-to-end measurements. Integrate the best valid measured candidate; do not assume component gains add together—measure any merged program against its inputs and retain it only if it is correct and faster. Preserve the generator, raw sorted-integer MD5 calculation, and output behavior; do not use expected answers or specialize to a test size.","analysis":"The official task is recursive quicksort of positive integers, with input N determining a reproducibly generated array and output being the MD5 of that array after sorting. The provided source identifies generation, sorting, sortedness checking, and checksum as separate costs, so each research lane keeps generation and checksum semantics fixed while testing a distinct sorting approach. This run’s baseline is valid and correct, with score 0.5 and mean time 0.07024732045829296 seconds; there is no selected parent or prior candidate history, and one diagnostic was unavailable. These measurements do not isolate the bottleneck or establish an optimization. The target is ARM64 with four CPU cores and no GPU."}